.TH MV 1 "2026-05-24" "substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mv \- move (rename) files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mv
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
[\fB\-T\fR]
\fISOURCE\fR \fIDEST\fR
.br
.B mv
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
\fISOURCE\fR... \fIDIRECTORY\fR
.br
.B mv
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
\fB\-t\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR \fISOURCE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Rename \fISOURCE\fR to \fIDEST\fR, or move \fISOURCE\fR(s) into
\fIDIRECTORY\fR.  Implements the full POSIX.1\-2024 surface plus
the BSD and GNU extensions enumerated below; where the two conflict,
substrate follows the BSD behaviour.
.PP
With no
.B \-T
flag, an existing
.I DEST
that is a directory causes
.I SOURCE
to be moved
.I into
that directory.  With
.B \-T
the destination is taken as a final name even when it exists as a
directory, allowing atomic directory replacement.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Force.  Do not prompt before overwriting.  Last of
.BR \-f / \-i / \-n
on the command line wins (POSIX).
.TP
.B \-i
Interactive.  Prompt before overwriting an existing target.
.TP
.B \-n
No\-clobber.  Refuse to overwrite an existing target, silently.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose.  Print one line per successful operation in the form
\fBrenamed '\fISRC\fB' -> '\fIDEST\fB'\fR.
.TP
.B \-h
BSD.  When \fIDEST\fR is a symbolic link to a directory, treat the
link itself as the destination (replace the link, do not traverse
into it).
.TP
.BI \-S " SUFFIX"
Use \fISUFFIX\fR (default \fB~\fR, or \fB$SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX\fR
if set) as the simple\-backup suffix.
.TP
\fB\-\-backup\fR[=\fICONTROL\fR], \fB\-b\fR
GNU.  Make a backup of an existing target before overwriting.
\fICONTROL\fR is one of \fBnone\fR/\fBoff\fR, \fBnumbered\fR/\fBt\fR,
\fBexisting\fR/\fBnil\fR, or \fBsimple\fR/\fBnever\fR.  \fB\-b\fR
alone honours \fB$VERSION_CONTROL\fR (defaulting to \fBexisting\fR).
.TP
.BI \-t " DIRECTORY"
GNU.  Move all \fISOURCE\fR operands into \fIDIRECTORY\fR.  Useful
with \fBxargs\fR.
.TP
.B \-T
GNU.  Treat \fIDEST\fR as a normal file even if it is an existing
directory.  Enables atomic directory replacement via
.BR rename (2).
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-trailing\-slashes
GNU.  Strip trailing slashes from \fISOURCE\fR operands before
deriving their basename.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR[=\fIWHEN\fR]
GNU.  Move only when \fISOURCE\fR is newer than \fIDEST\fR
(\fBolder\fR, the default), never (\fBnone\fR), never with
diagnostic (\fBnone\-fail\fR), or always (\fBall\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-debug
GNU.  Implies \fB\-\-verbose\fR plus additional preservation
diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit \fB0\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the implementation version and exit \fB0\fR.
.SH CROSS\-FILESYSTEM MOVES
When
.BR rename (2)
fails with
.BR EXDEV ,
\fBmv\fR falls back to a
.BR cp (1)\-style
copy followed by an
.BR rm (1)\-style
remove of the source.  Modes, ownership, and timestamps are
preserved where the underlying syscalls permit; symbolic links are
moved as-is (never dereferenced).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX
Default simple\-backup suffix, overriding the built-in \fB~\fR.
.TP
.B VERSION_CONTROL
Default backup style when \fB\-b\fR is given without an explicit
\fI\-\-backup=CONTROL\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fB0\fR on full success.  Non\-zero if any operand failed.
.SH STANDARDS
Conforms to POSIX.1-2024
.BR mv (1).
The
.BR \-h ", " \-v ", " \-i ", " \-n
options are also POSIX (the latter three were carried over from
prior editions).  The
.B \-h
flag and the EXDEV fallback follow FreeBSD semantics.  The
.BR \-b ", " \-S ", " \-t ", " \-T ", " \-\-backup ", " \-\-update ", " \-\-strip\-trailing\-slashes
flags are GNU coreutils extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR rename (2),
.BR renameat2 (2),
.BR symlink (2)
